{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And he lifted his eyes, etc.</b> We need to know why it was necessary to say <b>and behold</b>, and also why it was necessary to say <b>standing</b> — presumably they were standing, so what do we gain from this information? Further, its saying <b>upon him</b> has no meaning, and if you say it means near him, this is contradicted by its saying ״וַיָּרָץ לִקְרָאתָם״. It is further difficult that it says a second time ״וַיַּרְא״ and was not satisfied with the first one. Further, for what purpose did Abraham bow down to the guests before knowing from them that they were angels, and why was he not concerned about Arabs, etc.?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent of the verse is according to what was revealed at the end of the matter, that they were angels, and they came in the likeness of men to lodge with Abraham for known reasons. Therefore it says <b>and he saw, and behold,</b> etc. — meaning: what appeared to him were men, yet they were not so. And had it said ״וַיַּרְא שְׁלֹשָׁה אֲנָשִׁים״ it would be speaking falsehood, heaven forbid." "And its saying <b>standing</b> is to say that Abraham, through his familiarity with the ministers on high, recognized in them that they were appointed over a matter — meaning, he recognized in them that they were sent to Abraham for his needs, as indeed was the case: one to heal him, one to announce ״כָּעֵת חַיָּה״ etc., and one — although he was going to overturn Sodom, nevertheless for the love of Abraham he stood held back from going until HaShem spoke to him and what happened happened, and then he turned and went on his way. See then that all their standing was for his sake." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes in this manner: <b>men standing</b> means standing in the likeness of men; <b>upon him</b> means for his sake, for the purpose of hosting guests. And he recognized in them that they were angels of Elohim, for the form of an angel cannot be concealed. Go out and learn from Manoah's wife (Judges 13:13), and all the more so Abraham, whose familiarity with them was from the past. And its saying <b>and he saw</b> might appear to mean that this second seeing was his recognition that they were angels; but there is no need for this, since the verse already alluded that he recognized them. Rather, the verse informs us that in the seeing with which he saw them he was healed of his pain, and he arose and ran toward them — for the angel is perceived from afar, and there is no difference in the dimension of spirituality to prevent the healing, and immediately and at once Raphael fulfilled his mission and healed him. And from this awareness, ״וַיִּשְׁתַּחוּ אָרְצָה״ — he bowed down to the ministers on high."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:51.858316219Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}